---
/**
 * DETAIL ROUTE — /services/[slug]
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Generated from the shared data files, so adding a service creates its route
 * automatically — no new page file needed.
 *
 * TWO KINDS OF PAGE, ONE ROUTE
 *   A slug with a written page renders it (`ServiceDetailPage`); a slug without
 *   one renders the reservation shell (`ReservationPage`). The branch is driven
 *   by `detailPageFor()`, so building a page for real is: write its data file,
 *   add it to `detailPages` in `src/data/service-detail.js`, and remove its path
 *   from `builtDetailPaths`' siblings in `src/data/routes.js` — the reservation
 *   `planned` copy for a built slug is never rendered, so a stale reservation
 *   entry is inert rather than misleading.
 *
 * WHY NOT ONE FILE PER SERVICE
 *   A static `src/pages/services/<slug>.astro` would also work, but then the
 *   route list lives in two places (the file system and the data registry) and
 *   the reservation fallback has to be duplicated for every service still to be
 *   written. One route, two branches, one registry.
 *
 * THE COST OF ONE ROUTE FOR TWO BRANCHES — a stated trade-off, not an oversight
 *   Because both shells are imported statically, Astro links BOTH branches'
 *   stylesheets on EVERY `/services/<slug>` page. Measured in the build:
 *
 *     /services/product-sourcing   24.1 core + 3.5 inner-pages + 4.9 shared + 6.1 detail
 *     /services/quality-control    24.1 core + 3.5 inner-pages + 4.9 shared + 6.1 detail
 *
 *   So a built page ships `inner-pages.css` (the reservation shell's sheet)
 *   without using it, and a reservation page ships the two detail sheets without
 *   using them. The alternative — a second route file per branch — would put the
 *   same path in two route tables, and Astro's static-over-dynamic precedence
 *   would then decide which one wins silently. One deterministic route is worth
 *   ~1 KB of gzipped CSS that is already cached site-wide: `inner-pages.css` is
 *   also the sheet for `/industries`, `/insights` and `/case-studies`, so a
 *   visitor who reaches a service page has almost certainly downloaded it
 *   already. The reservation-page half of the leak disappears as pages 2–5 are
 *   written, and those pages are `noindex` meanwhile.
 *
 *   If this ever stops being acceptable, the fix is to move the two stylesheet
 *   imports out of the shells and into per-branch page files — not to inline
 *   rules or to merge the sheets.
 *
 * INDEXABILITY
 *   The reservation branch is `noindex` and absent from sitemap.xml. A built
 *   page is indexable — its path is subtracted from `detailRoutePaths` in
 *   `src/data/routes.js`, which is what drives both the `<meta name="robots">`
 *   split and the sitemap filter. Forgetting that subtraction leaves a finished,
 *   content-rich page excluded from search; `npm run check` cross-checks the two
 *   signals and fails the build if a page is noindex but listed, or indexable
 *   but absent.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import ReservationPage from '../../components/ReservationPage.astro';
import ServiceDetailPage from '../../components/services/ServiceDetailPage.astro';

import { detailPageFor } from '../../data/service-detail.js';
import { detailRouteGroups, reservationForDetail } from '../../data/routes.js';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return detailRouteGroups['services'].map((entry) => {
    const detail = detailPageFor(entry.slug);

    return {
      params: { slug: entry.slug },
      props: detail
        ? { detail }
        : {
            detail: null,
            page: reservationForDetail(entry.kind, entry.item, entry.parent),
          },
    };
  });
}

const { detail, page } = Astro.props;
---

{detail ? <ServiceDetailPage page={detail} /> : <ReservationPage {...page} />}
